    "argument_markdown": "## Source-backed inputs\n\nThe dependency package ATT-0066, confirmed independently by REV-0110 and REV-0116, supplies the following exact inputs. CLM-0066-1 gives deg L=6, deg M=4 for M=\u03c9^2\u2297W_5^\u2228, the identity h^0(L)=1+h^0(M), speciality of M, and h^0(M)\u22643. Under the weak relative-canonical normalization, CLM-0066-2 gives M\u2245\u03b3^*O(1), hence h^0(L)\u2208{3,4}. CLM-0066-4 and CLM-0066-5 give, on a reduced constant-h^0 stratum, local freeness and base change for p_*L and identify the global linear system with the closed Abel\u2013Jacobi incidence. The CE convention is sourced by SRC-0004; FND-0125 records the hyperelliptic CE calculation used below.\n\n## Proved steps\n\nFix a geometric fibre and suppose h^0(L)=4. The Riemann\u2013Roch identity from CLM-0066-1 gives h^0(M)=3. Since M is special of degree four, equality holds in Clifford's inequality. The nontrivial and noncanonical equality case forces C to be hyperelliptic and M to be twice its hyperelliptic pencil.\n\nLet h:C\u2192P^1 be the hyperelliptic double cover and let \u03b3:C\u2192P^1 have degree four. If h and \u03b3 were independent, Castelnuovo\u2013Severi would give g(C)\u2264(2\u22121)(4\u22121)=3, contrary to g(C)=6. Thus \u03b3=q\u2218h for a degree-two map q:P^1\u2192P^1.\n\nThis factorization cannot have balanced CE relations. Indeed h_*O_C=O\u2295O(\u22127), q_*O=O\u2295O(\u22121), and q_*O(\u22127)=O(\u22124)^2: the last bundle has rank two and degree \u22128, while twisting by O(3) gives no sections, forcing both summands to have degree \u22124. Hence \u03b3_*O_C=O\u2295O(\u22121)\u2295O(\u22124)^2 and the Tschirnhausen bundle is E=O(1)\u2295O(4)^2.\n\nFor completeness, the part of the FND-0125 relation calculation needed here is universal rather than a single-fibre experiment. Write A=h^*O(1). After choosing coordinates, every degree-two q is the model z=x^2. The CE quadratic-relation sequence twisted by O(\u22126) identifies H^0(F(\u22126)) with the kernel of H^0(Sym^2E(\u22126))\u2192H^0(C,A^6). From E=O(1)\u2295O(4)^2 one obtains h^0(Sym^2E(\u22126))=9, whereas h^0(C,A^6)=7. Therefore h^0(F(\u22126))\u22652. Balanced F=O(4)\u2295O(5) would instead give h^0(F(\u22126))=0, a contradiction. FND-0125 sharpens this calculation to F=O(2)\u2295O(7), but that stronger splitting is unnecessary here.\n\nThus no balanced fibre can satisfy h^0(L)=4, so B_4 is empty. The normalized ATT-0066 range h^0(L)\u2208{3,4} now gives h^0(L)=3 on every geometric fibre. Because B is reduced, CLM-0066-4 applies on the single stratum B=B_3: p_*L is locally free of rank three and commutes with base change. CLM-0066-5 then identifies the closed Abel\u2013Jacobi incidence with its relative projectivization, hence with a Zariski-locally trivial P^2-bundle. This eliminates only the proposed B_4/P^3 piece; it does not assert irreducibility or describe components inherited from B.\n\n## Computational hypotheses\n\nNone. No point count or finite experiment is used to establish the universal statement.\n\n## Remaining gaps and exact-target boundary\n\nThe weak relative-canonical normalization remains an explicit hypothesis. The proof does not establish nonemptiness of the reduced-support open, ordered Conf_6 incidence or full S_6 monodromy. It also gives no image dimension, closure, lci/Gysin class, Hodge purity, Tate support, or Tate assembly in M_{6,6}. In particular it does not prove that W_{\u221216}H^{BM}_{16}(M_{6,6}) is a direct sum of Q(8), so RED-0001 remains unresolved.",
